# tests/T01.bd - EXPERIMENT T01: clock and external-instrument
# validation. Run this BEFORE any other test in the CPU-load research
# programme: it validates the only instrument the rest of them have.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PURPOSE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every timing number this investigation has ever recorded - including the
# +40.7 / +111.0 / +40.3 / +65.5 / +64.3s crash spread the whole thing is
# chasing - comes from ONE instrument: get_unix_time() differences printed by
# the script under test. That instrument has never once been checked against
# anything outside itself. Two anomalies in our own notes say it needs it:
#   1. web_api.txt documents  get_unix_time() -> int  , yet every checkpoint
#      trace we have ever printed shows 1-4 decimal places.
#   2. Three separate runs landed at 98.6000s / 98.6020s / 98.6030s - a 3ms
#      spread over a ~100s run, across two unrelated code changes. Real wall
#      clocks do not do that. Tick-quantised simulated clocks do.
# If in-game elapsed time is simulated or scaled, then "the same script took
# 40.7s in one run and 111.0s in another" is a statement about simulated time
# and may not mean what the whole investigation has assumed it means.
#
# This test measures ONE interval with THREE mutually independent clocks
# (the script's own get_unix_time(); the terminal's `time` command; a physical
# stopwatch outside the game entirely), probes the clock's granularity at a
# resolution no stopwatch can reach, and opens two never-used external
# instruments: `time` (documented in help_processes.txt, never used once by
# this project, and the only instrument that costs the measured script zero
# ticks) and the ps/jobs column set.
#
# THERE IS NO get_cpu() ANYWHERE IN THIS SCRIPT, deliberately. get_cpu() is
# suspected of adding the load it reports; including it would contaminate the
# one interval three clocks are supposed to be measuring identically.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PACING NOTE (every line costs 1 tick; unpaced bursts kill scripts)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Clock mode: the only unpaced run of lines is the 5-sample granularity probe
# (5 lines - well under the ~10-line danger line), and the 6-step bookkeeping
# loop inside each timed round, which is preceded by sleep(1) every time.
# Every log() in this script is separated from the next by a sleep(1).
# Fatal mode: the 300-step unpaced burst IS THE EXPERIMENT - it is supposed to
# die. It is bounded at 300 iterations (~600 executed lines, ~60s at base tier)
# so that if the overload kill somehow does NOT fire, the run still ends on its
# own inside a minute instead of hanging.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT EACH POSSIBLE RESULT MEANS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# raw_clock_t0 line:
#   ~1.789e9 (i.e. 1789xxxxxx) in Sept 2026  -> it really is the Unix epoch,
#       the doc's "real wall-clock time" claim is literally true.
#   small / zero-based / any other magnitude -> it is an in-game clock wearing
#       a Unix name; every absolute time we have ever logged is in-game units.
#   printed with NO decimal point             -> the doc's "-> int" is right and
#       our fractional traces come from somewhere else (str() formatting, or
#       the subtraction, not the source).
#
# gran d1..d4 (four back-to-back get_unix_time() deltas, one line apart each):
#   ~0.10 with jitter (0.09-0.11)  -> lines really are paced at ~100ms each on
#       a base-tier CPU; the standing "1 line = 1 tick = 100ms" model is right
#       and the clock is real at that resolution.
#   EXACTLY 0.1 every time, to full float precision, or a 0 / 0 / 0.1 pattern
#       -> the clock is TICK-QUANTISED. It does not measure time, it counts
#       ticks. Every elapsed figure in field_notes.txt is a tick count divided
#       by an assumed rate, and the 98.6000/98.6020/98.6030 spread is explained.
#   ~0.00001-0.001 (far below 0.1) -> MAJOR: executing a line does NOT cost
#       100ms of wall time. Ticks are a budget enforced somewhere else, not a
#       per-line delay, and "50 unpaced lines = 5 seconds" is wrong - bursts
#       run at full speed and the overload arrives far sooner than we thought.
#   0.0 every time -> the clock's resolution is coarser than this test can see;
#       fall back on the interval/stopwatch comparison only.
#
# INTERVAL vs stopwatch (the ground truth - a stopwatch is the only clock in
# this whole test that is genuinely outside the game):
#   agree within 0.5s on a ~25s interval, both repeats -> in-game elapsed time
#       is real wall-clock time. Every number in field_notes.txt stands as is.
#   differ by more than 1.5s, same direction on both repeats -> in-game time is
#       simulated or scaled. Record the RATIO (stopwatch/interval); every
#       elapsed figure in field_notes.txt must be re-read in those units and
#       the rest of the research programme re-cut before it is run.
#
# `time` report vs total_from_first_line:
#   agree within ~1s -> `time` is a usable external instrument. NOTE it is an
#       IN-GAME command, so agreement proves CONSISTENCY, not reality - only
#       the stopwatch can prove reality.
#   differ by a fixed amount at both blocks=10 and blocks=20 -> that amount is
#       process start/teardown overhead; subtract it from now on.
#   differ proportionally (grows with run length) -> `time` and get_unix_time()
#       are running off different clocks, which is itself a major finding.
#   `time` refuses a script argument in both forms, or prints nothing for the
#       killed run -> no external instrument exists; crash time-of-death stays
#       an inference from the last checkpoint that printed, as it is today.
#
# fatal mode under `time`:
#   a duration is printed for a KILLED script -> we have gained an exact,
#       zero-observer time-of-death. `time` total minus the printed
#       burst_start gives the first DIRECT measurement of the overload kill
#       delay. Predicted ~5-7s (5s sustained at 100%, plus ~1s of ramp from
#       the near-idle baseline the two sleep(2)s leave behind). A materially
#       different number means the documented "5 straight seconds" rule is
#       wrong or is not measured the way we assume.
#
# ps / jobs columns:
#   any column beyond pid+name (cpu, load, ticks, runtime, state) -> the
#       aggregate get_cpu() becomes DECOMPOSABLE per process, and the
#       single-contributor assumption behind every reading we have is checkable.
#   pid and name only -> no per-process accounting exists; aggregate is all we
#       will ever get.
#
# concurrent foreground run while a background run is alive:
#   foreground interval >= 1.5x its solo value -> concurrent processes SHARE
#       ONE TICK BUDGET. Any stray background process is then a live candidate
#       for the run-to-run variance, and every future test must confirm `ps` is
#       clean first.
#   within 10% of solo -> budgets are independent; drop that candidate.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO RUN  (full procedure, ~5 min of wall clock in total; no single run is
# longer than ~60s. Do the steps in order; paste every line back verbatim.)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  STEP 0  `clear`, then `ps` and `jobs` - confirm nothing else is running and
#          PASTE BOTH OUTPUTS VERBATIM INCLUDING THE COLUMN HEADERS. (The
#          earlier ps/jobs check in this investigation recorded only "nothing
#          running" - it never captured the columns, which is half the point.)
#  STEP 1  `T01 10`
#          Have a phone stopwatch ready. Start it the instant the
#          "INTERVAL START" line appears; stop it the instant the
#          "INTERVAL END" line appears. Record stopwatch vs the printed
#          interval=. Also eyeball raw_clock_t0 against a real Unix timestamp
#          (any epoch converter, or your phone) - is it the real epoch?
#  STEP 2  Repeat STEP 1 exactly once more. Human reaction is worth about
#          +/-0.4s per end, so one run cannot distinguish a 0.8s divergence
#          from a mis-press; two can.
#  STEP 3  `time T01 10`
#          If the terminal rejects that form, try `time run T01 10`.
#          Record what `time` prints VERBATIM - its format and units are
#          completely undocumented and are themselves a finding. Compare it to
#          the printed total_from_first_line.
#  STEP 4  `time T01 20`
#          Two run lengths are what separates a constant startup overhead from
#          a proportional clock bias. One data point cannot.
#  STEP 5  `time T01 10 fatal`
#          This run is MEANT to die with "[SYSTEM] CPU overloaded". Record
#          whether `time` still reports a duration for a killed script, the
#          duration itself, and the last line the script printed.
#  STEP 6  Repeat STEP 5 once. Kill-delay variance is the central mystery of
#          this whole investigation; two samples cost 20 seconds.
#  STEP 7  `run T01 25 clock BG &`  (if `&` is rejected in that
#          form, try `T01 25 clock BG &`), then IMMEDIATELY
#          type `ps`, then `jobs`. Paste both verbatim with headers.
#  STEP 8  While the BG run is still printing, run `T01 10 clock FG`
#          in the foreground. Compare its interval= to STEP 1/2's. Every line
#          is tagged BG or FG so the interleaved output stays readable.
#          Afterwards `kill <pid>` the background job if it is still alive.
#
# REPEATS: STEP 1 twice, STEP 5 twice, everything else once. Repeat STEP 8
# twice only if it shows any slowdown at all.
#
# @param blocks "rounds of sleep(1)+fixed burst in the timed interval (10 = ~25s, 20 = ~50s)" default=10
# @param mode "clock (default) or fatal (deliberate overload, run it under `time`)" default=clock
# @param tag "label prefixed to every line, so a backgrounded run stays readable next to a foreground one" default=A

var B = int(str(get_param("blocks", "10")).replace("\"", "").trim())
var MODE = str(get_param("mode", "clock")).replace("\"", "").trim()
var TAG = "T01[" + str(get_param("tag", "A")).replace("\"", "").trim() + "]"
var t0 = get_unix_time()
log(TAG + " raw_clock_t0=" + str(t0) + " mode=" + MODE + " blocks=" + str(B))
sleep(1)

if MODE == "fatal":
    var f = 0
    log(TAG + " FATAL armed - a 300-step unpaced burst starts two lines below. This run is MEANT to die.")
    sleep(2)
    log(TAG + " FATAL burst_start=+" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s <- LAST LINE BEFORE THE BURST. `time` total minus this = seconds from burst start to kill.")
    while f < 300:
        f = f + 1
    log(TAG + " FATAL burst SURVIVED to +" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s - UNEXPECTED, report this verbatim")
    return

# --- granularity probe: 5 clock samples, exactly one line apart each ---------
# This is the only part of the test with resolution finer than human reaction
# time. It measures the cost of the measurement line itself, which is the
# cleanest calibration of "1 line = 1 tick" available anywhere.
var a1 = get_unix_time()
var a2 = get_unix_time()
var a3 = get_unix_time()
var a4 = get_unix_time()
var a5 = get_unix_time()
sleep(1)
log(TAG + " gran d1=" + str(a2 - a1))
sleep(1)
log(TAG + " gran d2=" + str(a3 - a2))
sleep(1)
log(TAG + " gran d3=" + str(a4 - a3))
sleep(1)
log(TAG + " gran d4=" + str(a5 - a4))
sleep(1)

# --- the three-clock interval: NOTHING is logged between the two markers -----
log(TAG + " INTERVAL START - START YOUR STOPWATCH NOW")
var t_i0 = get_unix_time()
var r = 0
var j = 0
while r < B:
    sleep(1)
    j = 0
    while j < 6:
        j = j + 1
    r = r + 1
var t_i1 = get_unix_time()
log(TAG + " INTERVAL END - STOP STOPWATCH NOW - interval=" + str(t_i1 - t_i0) + "s")
sleep(1)
log(TAG + " DONE blocks=" + str(B) + " interval=" + str(t_i1 - t_i0) + "s (vs stopwatch) total_from_first_line=" + str(get_unix_time() - t0) + "s (vs `time`)")
